### What is frequency counting with a HashMap?

Frequency counting means building a map from each distinct element to the number of times it appears in the array. You iterate over the array once, and for each element you either start its count at one or add one to the existing count. The result is a `HashMap<Integer, Integer>` where every key is a unique element and every value is that element's total occurrence count.

At the National Bank of India, Rajesh processes daily transaction records where each record carries a merchant category code. He needs to know how many transactions each category received so the fraud team can spot unusual spikes. A frequency map gives him that answer in a single pass.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyCounter {

    public static Map<Integer, Integer> countFrequencies(int[] transactions) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> frequency = new HashMap<>();
        for (int code : transactions) {
            frequency.merge(code, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        return frequency;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] merchantCodes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101, 404, 303};
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = countFrequencies(merchantCodes);
        System.out.println("Frequency map: " + freq);
    }
}
```

```text
Frequency map: {101=3, 202=2, 303=2, 404=1}
```

### Why use a HashMap instead of nested loops?

The brute-force approach counts each element by rescanning the entire array, which takes O(n^2) time. With a HashMap each lookup and insert is amortized O(1), so processing every element once gives O(n) total time. For Rajesh's fraud dashboard at the National Bank of India, where millions of transactions arrive daily, the quadratic approach would never finish within the reporting window.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class BruteForceFrequency {

    // Wrong approach: O(n^2) nested loop
    public static void countBruteForce(int[] arr) {
        boolean[] visited = new boolean[arr.length];
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (visited[i]) continue;
            int count = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                if (arr[i] == arr[j]) {
                    count++;
                    visited[j] = true;
                }
            }
            System.out.println(arr[i] + " appears " + count + " times");
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] codes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101};
        countBruteForce(codes);
    }
}
```

```text
101 appears 3 times
202 appears 2 times
303 appears 1 times
```

The brute-force version works but is far too slow for production-scale data.

### How does the merge method work internally?

The `merge` method on `HashMap` takes three arguments: the key, a default value, and a `BiFunction` that combines the existing value with the default when the key is already present. Calling `map.merge(key, 1, Integer::sum)` means: if the key is absent, insert it with value one; if the key is present, replace its value with `oldValue + 1`. This single line handles both the new-key and existing-key cases without an explicit if-else.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MergeDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> ledger = new HashMap<>();

        // First occurrence: key absent, inserts "HDFC" -> 1
        ledger.merge("HDFC", 1, Integer::sum);
        System.out.println("After first HDFC: " + ledger);

        // Second occurrence: key present, updates to 1 + 1 = 2
        ledger.merge("HDFC", 1, Integer::sum);
        System.out.println("After second HDFC: " + ledger);

        ledger.merge("ICICI", 1, Integer::sum);
        System.out.println("After first ICICI: " + ledger);
    }
}
```

```text
After first HDFC: {HDFC=1}
After second HDFC: {HDFC=2}
After first ICICI: {HDFC=2, ICICI=1}
```

### How is merge different from getOrDefault?

Both approaches build the same frequency map, but `merge` does the read-modify-write in a single atomic-like call, while `getOrDefault` requires a separate `put`. In concurrent contexts, `merge` on a `ConcurrentHashMap` is thread-safe for this operation, whereas the get-then-put pattern with a plain `HashMap` is not.

| Aspect | `merge` | `getOrDefault` + `put` |
|---|---|---|
| Lines of code | One call | Two calls (get then put) |
| Thread safety on ConcurrentHashMap | Atomic | Not atomic without synchronization |
| Readability | Slightly more abstract | More explicit |
| Performance | Same amortized O(1) | Same amortized O(1) |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class GetOrDefaultDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] merchantCodes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101};

        // Approach 1: merge
        Map<Integer, Integer> freqMerge = new HashMap<>();
        for (int code : merchantCodes) {
            freqMerge.merge(code, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        // Approach 2: getOrDefault
        Map<Integer, Integer> freqGet = new HashMap<>();
        for (int code : merchantCodes) {
            freqGet.put(code, freqGet.getOrDefault(code, 0) + 1);
        }

        System.out.println("merge result:         " + freqMerge);
        System.out.println("getOrDefault result:  " + freqGet);
    }
}
```

```text
merge result:         {101=3, 202=2, 303=1}
getOrDefault result:  {101=3, 202=2, 303=1}
```

### How do you find the most frequent element?

Once the frequency map is built, finding the most frequent element is a single scan of the entries comparing values. Rajesh uses this at the National Bank of India to find the busiest merchant category and flag it if it exceeds a threshold.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class MostFrequent {

    public static int findMostFrequent(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (int val : arr) {
            freq.merge(val, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        int maxCount = 0;
        int maxElement = arr[0];
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() > maxCount) {
                maxCount = entry.getValue();
                maxElement = entry.getKey();
            }
        }
        return maxElement;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] codes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101, 404, 303};
        System.out.println("Most frequent: " + findMostFrequent(codes));
    }
}
```

```text
Most frequent: 101
```

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is forgetting to handle the first occurrence of a key, which causes a `NullPointerException` when trying to increment a null value.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyMistake {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] codes = {101, 202, 101};
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();

        // WRONG: NullPointerException on first occurrence
        // freq.put(101, freq.get(101) + 1);  // get(101) returns null

        // CORRECT: use getOrDefault or merge
        for (int code : codes) {
            freq.put(code, freq.getOrDefault(code, 0) + 1);
        }
        System.out.println(freq);
    }
}
```

```text
{101=2, 202=1}
```

Another mistake is using `containsKey` followed by `get`, which traverses the hash bucket twice for the same key. The `getOrDefault` or `merge` approach is cleaner and avoids the redundant lookup.

### What about using Java Streams?

Java Streams provide a functional approach to frequency counting using `Collectors.groupingBy` with `Collectors.counting()`. This is concise but creates intermediate objects and is generally slower than a plain loop for large arrays.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class StreamFrequency {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] merchantCodes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 101};

        Map<Integer, Long> freq = Arrays.stream(merchantCodes)
                .boxed()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Function.identity(),
                        Collectors.counting()
                ));

        System.out.println("Stream frequency: " + freq);
    }
}
```

```text
Stream frequency: {101=3, 202=2, 303=1}
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty array, an array with a single element, and an array where all elements are the same.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FrequencyEdgeCases {

    public static Map<Integer, Integer> countFrequencies(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (int val : arr) {
            freq.merge(val, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        return freq;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Edge case 1: empty array
        System.out.println("Empty: " + countFrequencies(new int[]{}));

        // Edge case 2: single element
        System.out.println("Single: " + countFrequencies(new int[]{500}));

        // Edge case 3: all same
        System.out.println("All same: " + countFrequencies(new int[]{101, 101, 101}));

        // Edge case 4: all distinct
        System.out.println("All distinct: " + countFrequencies(new int[]{101, 202, 303}));
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: {}
Single: {500=1}
All same: {101=3}
All distinct: {101=1, 202=1, 303=1}
```

### What problems build on the frequency map?

The frequency map is a foundational building block for many interview problems. Finding the first non-repeating element means scanning the map for the first entry with count one in insertion order using a `LinkedHashMap`. Detecting the majority element means checking if any count exceeds n/2. Grouping anagrams means building a frequency signature as the key. Checking whether two arrays are permutations means comparing their frequency maps for equality.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class FirstNonRepeating {

    public static int findFirstNonRepeating(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> freq = new LinkedHashMap<>();
        for (int val : arr) {
            freq.merge(val, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (Map.Entry<Integer, Integer> entry : freq.entrySet()) {
            if (entry.getValue() == 1) {
                return entry.getKey();
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] codes = {101, 202, 101, 303, 202, 404};
        System.out.println("First non-repeating: " + findFirstNonRepeating(codes));
    }
}
```

```text
First non-repeating: 303
```

### What about thread safety and concurrency?

In a multi-threaded environment such as a real-time transaction processing system, a plain `HashMap` is not thread-safe. Using `ConcurrentHashMap` with `merge` provides atomic frequency updates without external synchronization.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentMap;

public class ConcurrentFrequency {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ConcurrentMap<Integer, Integer> freq = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        // Simulating concurrent updates from multiple teller threads
        int[] batch1 = {101, 202, 101};
        int[] batch2 = {202, 303, 101};

        // merge on ConcurrentHashMap is atomic
        for (int code : batch1) {
            freq.merge(code, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        for (int code : batch2) {
            freq.merge(code, 1, Integer::sum);
        }

        System.out.println("Concurrent frequency: " + freq);
    }
}
```

```text
Concurrent frequency: {101=3, 202=2, 303=1}
```
